#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""gcs2sfz -- Render imscomp --sfzpipecsv instrument CSVs to an audio mixdown.

Usage:
    gcs2sfz <csv-or-dir> [--outdir DIR] [--sfzdir DIR] [--samplerate N] [--sf2 FILE]

Pipeline:
    <instrument>.csv  (start_sec,dur_sec,midi_note,velocity,pan,reverb, one per line)
        -> <instrument>.mid                  (per-instrument standard MIDI file)
        -> ~/bin/sfizz_render <VPO>.sfz      (per-instrument WAV)
        -> ffmpeg amix                        (one mixed WAV)

The 10th CSV column (optional) is the per-note articulation token from
imscomp --sfzpipecsv -- see "Articulation" below.

Pan and reverb come verbatim from the score. Pan (MIDI CC10, 0..127) is written
into the MIDI and honored by both sfizz (CC10->pan by default) and fluidsynth.
Reverb (MIDI CC91, 0 dry..127) is honored natively by the fluidsynth GM
fallback; for the sfizz/VPO path the instrument WAV gets an offline aecho
early-reflection room tail scaled by reverb/127 (the ffmpeg here has a broken
afir that mutes the dry signal, and this sfizz build has no reverb effect).

For 1812-specific one-shots (gunshot/explosion/canon) the mapping points at
EFFECTS/*.wav files at the library root; those are placed directly at their
CSV note times via ffmpeg (trimmed to the scored duration, velocity-scaled).

Instruments that have no VPO/one-shot mapping (e.g. the default "Acoustic
Grand Piano", custom names like "Bell Tower") fall back to GM: their CSV is
rendered through fluidsynth + GeneralUser.sf2 (--sf2), so default-piano pieces
still produce audio instead of being silently skipped.

Loudness balance: sample libraries differ widely in recorded level (VPO brass
sustains sit far above the soft NoBudget woodwinds), so a per-instrument gain
table is available via the GCS2SFZ_GAIN env var as "name:db,name:db":
    GCS2SFZ_GAIN="flute:6,french horn:-4"  gcs2sfz <csv-or-dir>
Gains (dB, positive louder) are applied to each rendered stem before the
mixdown so the melody isn't buried by the texture.

Articulation: the VPO sustain patches attack over 0.2-0.6s and release over
0.6-2.25s, blurring fast melodic runs. imscomp's --sfzpipecsv writes a 10th
per-note articulation column (staccato/marcato/tenuto/accent/legato/tied/
sustain) straight from the score's suffixes; staccato and marcato notes render
with a staccato patch, everything else stays on the sustain patch. CSVs
without the column (older 9-col output) fall back to a note-length split
(GCS2SFZ_ARTIC_MAX) and render short notes as staccato blended under a
fast-attack sustain (GCS2SFZ_ARTIC_BLEND + GCS2SFZ_ATTACK), giving crisp
onsets on a connected, sustained line:

    GCS2SFZ_ARTIC_MAX    note-length split threshold (s), default 0.5, 0 disa-
                         bles (only used when the CSV has no articulation col)
    GCS2SFZ_ARTIC_BLEND  layer sustain under staccato short notes, default 1
    GCS2SFZ_ATTACK       sustain-patch ampeg_attack override (s), default 0.03
    GCS2SFZ_GM_GAIN      fluidsynth master gain for GM-fallback stems, default
                         0.5 -- matches the "synth.gain 0.5" imscomp emits in
                         the .fs.  Fluidsynth's default is 0.2, which would
                         otherwise make every GM-fallback piece (default piano,
                         bell tower, ...) ~8 dB quieter than its GM render.
    GCS2SFZ_GM_EXPR      GM-fallback expression exponent (default 1.3): the
                         note-on velocity is scaled by (expression/127)^E to
                         fold the skipped per-note CC11 into velocity.  Higher
                         deepens the soft dynamics (toward the .fs render).

Requirements: python3, ~/bin/sfizz_render, fluidsynth, ffmpeg, and the VPO
library (plus GeneralUser.sf2 for GM fallback).
"""

